A1. The outlook for this is rather shaky. ~ PORTHOLE
A9. You will always find a good bed and running water at this place in the mountains. ~ STREAM
A14. A passage at arms (plural). ~ ARMHOLES
A15. This always comes intact. ~ TA
A16. The tale of a fish. ~ LIE
A17. Half silk. ~ SI
A18. This is something you never find in the best circles. ~ END
A19. All the humor in this is exhausted. ~ SPENT
A22. This will make you hum. ~ MM
A23. A low-down joint. ~ TOE
A25. Sometimes this gets full of liquor and stands on its head. ~ KEG
A27. This fellow thought he had a golden opportunity but it turned out to be only a myth. ~ MIDAS
A29. You often see the heads of this order hanging around hunting lodges. ~ ELKS
A31. This continues far, far into the night. ~ NAG
A33. A drink fit for the gods. ~ NECTAR
A35. Percolates. ~ SEEPS
A37. The part of the present that never was sent. ~ PRE
A39. What Capone left in Florida. ~ MIAMI
A40. This long-haired fellow came from Asia for zoological research work. ~ YAK
A42. A boat that came from Arkansas. ~ ARK
A44. There is always some sap in this since the latest endurance tests have started. ~ TREE
A45. What preferred stock has done for the nation. ~ FED
A48. When a shark gets the head of a woman, look out for this. ~ MERMAID
A51. Measures adopted by the printers' union to encourage puzzle construction. ~ ENS
A52. In Illinois they take this for Champaign. ~ ROAD
A54. A tropical fruit. ~ YAM
A55. What aviators sometimes take before going up. ~ DIP
A57. The way an innocent man gets into a poker game. ~ ANTES
A59. This ruined the bridge. ~ GAB
A61. A small rodent to a Seotehman—a large ruminant to an American. ~ MOOSE
A65. The way the checks come in the last of the month. ~ USEFUL
A67. This definition is crude. ~ RAW
A69. The present atrocities. ~ TIES
A70. You will find this in "the flowers that bloom in the spring, tra-la.' ~ SEPAL
A72. This made a large cargo for one of the transit companies. ~ GAS
A74. The finish that was put on the Cadillac. ~ LAC
A75. What Caesar said "tu Brute." ~ ET
A77. One thing that Lindbergh does not possess. ~ RIVAL
A79. Now get this straight, please-this bar was found in a dry state. ~ ROD
A81. This was about half sober. ~ SO
A82. The end of vacation. ~ ION
A84. We think this is the end of Capone. ~ NE
A85. This is a phoney one. ~ OPERATOR
A88. The ties that bind. ~ NOOSES
A89. This story makes a good cellar. ~ BASEMENT

D1. These are toothsome things. ~ PASTES
D2. The class colors of this are orange and black. ~ ORIOLE
D3. There is not much room in this part of the farm. ~ RM
D4. An article of the Constitution. ~ THE
D5. Any goose can answer this. ~ HONK
D6. Out of date. ~ OLDEN
D7. When this was at the end of the table, in the midst of silence, it started lecturing. ~ LE
D8. Right at the bend they found the road in this shape. ~ ESS
D9. This seems to be the Maine song of the day. ~ STEIN
D10. Just one "dem" thing after another. ~ TANDEM
D11. This is in a fare way to get one into a high position. ~ EL
D12. This was put in for a purpose. ~ AIM
D13. Some sweet things they brought home from Scotland. ~ MEMORIES
D20. Call it half a day and quit. ~ PM
D21. Implied but not expressed. ~ TACIT
D24. This sounds as if it was going to be economical. ~ EKE
D26. A parting word. ~ GAP
D28. What one will do into space for that idea they are looking for. ~ STARE
D30. A watch word. ~ SPY
D32. Study this and find out your complex and mood, how singular and tense you are, how long your sentence will be, and when to make a dash for freedom. ~ GRAMMAR
D34. Verily this is the way that amendment started. ~ AMEN
D36. We will tell you about this Insullater. ~ SAM
D38. This is where you started erasing. ~ ERA
D41. Here is your solution. (It is less than 50% whiskey.) ~ KEY
D43. Don't let them do this to you. ~ KID
D45. Maid in Germany. ~ FRAULEIN
D46. This is the time they took to finish their luncheon (plur.). ~ EONS
D47. These will come in the course of time. ~ DATES
D49. The man that finally won the suit. ~ RAG
D50. This meaning is rather obscure. ~ DIM
D53. What a good puzzle will do to your troubles. ~ DEFER
D56. What an innocent-looking man often gets out of a poker game. ~ POT
D58. Lying with a straight face upward. ~ SUPINE
D60. The kind of a pipe a Scotchman likes. ~ BAG
D62. This acts as if it had been well drilled. ~ OIL
D63. What most fishermen like to fish in. ~ SEASON
D64. Grandmother's boy friend. ~ ESCORT
D66. What Hilda does when she bane told to take a bath. ~ LAVES
D68. These are their stock in trade. ~ WARES
D71. That high musical note that comes in last. ~ LA
D73. The way the farmer feels about his corn. ~ SORE
D76. No trouble here—the definition is more than necessary. ~ TOO
D78. A highball that was served in court. ~ LOB
D80. Beg your pardon, but there was some damage here. ~ DAM
D83. This was all we could get from a Norsk. ~ NO
D86. Ma's greatest worry. ~ PA
D87. Don't be discouraged—this always comes in late. ~ TE


